  "notes": "\"The Pen\" — a hook-first topline writer with track-arrangement co-credit, 2004–2010, sitting inside the crunk&B band at 84–105 with the sourced spec **G minor at 105 BPM in common time**, which is the mode. The research is explicit that \"he co-arranges rather than programs from scratch\", so the drum deltas are the crunk kit he arranges: an 808/909 kick with `lockTo808` at 0.55, a hard clap stacked flat on 2 and 4 with `offGridMs` 0, and \"continuous 16th hats with triplet rolls, and a long snare-roll crescendo into the hook\" — a 0.64 hat fill density with a `16T`/`32T`-weighted roll vocabulary at 0.42 per bar plus a `snareRoll` ladder carrying `buildAndStopProb`, which is what a crescendo written to stop for a hook is. His identifying contribution is not the kit at all, and the research names it: \"the **eight-note keyboard vamp** class of hook — a short, fixed, instantly-memorable synth cell repeated for the whole record, with the vocal hook built as a chant answering it\". That is encoded twice over — as a countermelody at a 0.66 density led by `arp` (the vamp is the counter), and as a `call_response` melody with a 0.9 step share, a 3–5 pitch motif, `twoBarCellRepeat` and staccato articulation (the chant answers it). \"Harmony reduced to one or two chords\" gives a single-chord top family and a 0.56 triad share. The low end is \"sustained 808 sub under synth stabs, tuned to the vamp root\", so `drums.bass808` takes role `bassline` and there is no `bassline` block. ⚠ Confidence is medium: the research rates the hook-construction parameter medium-high but the drum specifics only medium, and flags that `crunk` may have claimed him. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `Gm`, and the remaining values `Cm`, `Fm`, `Dm`, together with all 4 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry. ⚠ **`chords.harmonicRhythm` re-weighted (2026-08-17, harmony variety floor).** The entry gives the one-chord and the two-chord bed equal billing — \"a short, fixed, instantly-memorable synth cell repeated for the whole record ... Harmony reduced to one or two chords\" — and the engine's `vamp` value is *one* chord held for the whole clip (`generators::chords`: \"One chord for the whole pattern\"), so a `vamp` weight of 6-of-8 made the two-chord half of that sentence almost unreachable. `vamp` keeps the top weight; the two-chord reading is now reachable at both rates. ⚠ The split between those two rates is **INFERRED** — the entry names the device, not its rate.",
